{
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "info": {
    "title": "micronaut-crud-backend static-contract baseline",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "x-basis": "Hand-written from the pinned source nomemory/micronaut-crud-backend @ 14921d7bd0475f2c25c57a2b5cc796edbc9ba3e1 (master), independent of scanner output. Micronaut 2.1.3, Java 11, javax.* (not jakarta), H2 in-memory JPA, micronaut-security-jwt, ModelMapper 2.3.0. Both controllers are annotated @Secured(SecurityRule.IS_ANONYMOUS), so no Authorization header / 401 contract is asserted despite the JWT bearer config in application.yml. Success responses are built with HttpResponse.ok()/ok().body(...) and therefore return 200 (not 201). Absent entities use HttpResponse.notFound() with no body; Micronaut 2.x emits no RFC7807 problem body here. DELETE success is HttpResponse.ok() with no body. The request/response DTOs carry no javax.validation annotations, so no field is required and no validation 400 contract exists; the JSON body itself is required. createAuthor's single POJO parameter has no @Body annotation; Micronaut implicitly binds the one unannotated POJO parameter of a body-bearing route as the JSON body. Response wire shape equals the ModelMapper target DTO's declared properties (modelMapper.map(entity, XxxDTO.class) maps same-named bean properties recursively); the JPA entities Author/Book are never expanded, so entity-only state and the many-to-many graph do not leak. BookDTO.AuthorsInfo declares only id/firstName/lastName (no books), which cuts the Author<->Book relationship cycle on the wire. Path id is java.lang.Long -> integer/int64. Jackson runs with the default inclusion (no @JsonInclude / global serialization-inclusion config in the project); key presence is therefore ALWAYS in principle, but the scanner does not yet model a Java response-serialization required direction (consistent with the Spring gate), so response field required arrays are deliberately not asserted here and tracked as a generic Java enhancement rather than sample-specific behavior."
  },
